# Folder structure

Follow common **best practices** for project layout. Do not dump new files into a single flat directory.

## Core principles

- **Group by feature/domain**, not by dumping unrelated files together. Prefer `billing/invoices/createInvoice.ts` over `billingCreateInvoice.ts` at the parent level.
- **One concern per folder.** Split routes, services, data access, UI, and shared utilities into separate directories when they grow.
- **Colocate related files.** Keep a component with its styles, tests, and small helpers in the same folder (e.g. `Button/Button.tsx`, `Button.module.scss`, `Button.test.ts`).
- **Shallow, meaningful depth.** Use 2–4 levels when needed; avoid both “everything in one folder” and unnecessarily deep nesting.
- **Consistent naming.** Use kebab-case or camelCase/PascalCase consistently with the surrounding codebase; folder names should describe *what*, not *how* (`export/`, `voice/`, not `helpers2/`).
- **Public surface via index files** only when it improves imports; do not create barrel files that re-export entire subtrees without reason.

## When adding or splitting code

- Before creating a file, inspect nearby code and **follow existing conventions** in that part of the repo.
- When extracting from a large file, create a **dedicated subfolder** for the new module(s), not another sibling in an already crowded directory.
- If a directory has many unrelated files, **introduce subfolders** before adding more.

## Typical layout (adapt to stack)

| Layer | Best practice |
|---|---|
| HTTP/API | Thin route handlers; no business logic |
| Business logic | `services/`, `use-cases/`, or feature folders |
| Data | `queries/`, `repositories/`, or `data/` per domain |
| UI | Feature folders with local `components/`, `hooks/`, `utils/` |
| Tests | Colocated `__tests__/` or `*.test.ts` next to source |
| Shared | `lib/`, `utils/`, `types/` only for truly cross-cutting code |

## Avoid

- Flat directories with dozens of mixed-purpose files.
- Generic catch-all folders (`misc/`, `temp/`, `stuff/`) instead of a clear domain name.
- Deep paths that mirror every class name (`a/b/c/d/e/file.ts`) without organizational benefit.
